.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 208 AM PDT Sun Aug 2 2026
(Today and tonight)

An upper level disturbance will pass over the PacNW today, with
quasi-zonal flow to weak troughing setting up over northern CA. To
our southwest west, high pressure is being suppressed over the Desert
Southwest and southern CA. What does that mean for us in the short
term? Well, it means the marine layer will remain compressed as a
result, at about 500ft and hugging the San Mateo and Central Coast
line which means cloudy skies, patchy dense fog, and perhaps coastal
drizzle through the mid morning. Skies will then clear, leading to a
mainly sunny day across the region. Moderate HeatRisk is expected
today for interior locations where temperatures reach the mid 90s to
low 100s. Elsewhere, temperatures rise into the low 70s to mid 80s
unless you`re immediately along the coast where temperatures 60s.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 208 AM PDT Sun Aug 2 2026
(Monday through Saturday)

Little change for Monday as the aforementioned upper level trough
pushes east, allowing the pattern to change from quasi-zonal and
troughy to quasi-zonal with high pressure building in. The forecast
will essentially be on repeat, with perhaps interior temperatures
warming up a few degrees. Heading into  midweek, there are a few
items that could affect temperatures. The first being onshore flow
ushering in some cooler air; however, it will be dependent on how
quickly the high builds in. If it is able to build, 850mb temps look
to be fairly warm in the deterministic guidance. This means temps
may need to be bumped up a few degrees inland instead of showing a
subtle cooling trend. Beyond that high pressure looks to build and
hold leading to a warming trend for late week and into the
weekend.

