.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 452 AM PDT Sat Aug 1 2026

Near critical fire weather conditions are expected through the
weekend and potentially into next week. Temperatures remain around
10 to 15 degrees above normal across the interior through early
next week with highs in the upper 80s to 90s. No major changes to
the forecasted dry conditions. Daytime humidity retention remains
poor across the interior (25-30%) with humidity values dropping
into the 10s across the interior Central Coast, East Bay
Hills/Valleys, and the higher elevations. Portions of the interior
Central Coast may see daytime humidity values drop into the single
digits this weekend into early next week. Overnight RH recovery
remains poor at locations above 2,000 feet with values between 25
to 40% expected with some favored locations struggling to reach
even 25%. This will be particularly true across the Mayacamas,
highest peaks of the East Bay Hills, the Diablo Range, the Santa
Cruz Mountains, the Santa Lucia and Gabilan Ranges. Nocturnal
drying (rapid decrease in RH through the pre- dawn hours) may also
transpire which will increase the burn period or time in which
fire behavior may remain active. Energy Release Components (ERC)
are projected to climb above the 97th percentile across the
interior Central Coast and Mid Coast to Mendocino (North Bay)
zone. ERC values are rapidly climbing across the Diablo-Santa
Cruz zone (Diablo Mountains and Santa Cruz Mountains) with values
approaching the 97th percentile by next week. Diurnally breezy
onshore winds are expected along the coast, mountain gaps and
passes, and along the ridgelines each afternoon/evening. With
near critically dry conditions and above normal temperatures
expected, individuals across these areas are advised to avoid
activities that may spark a fire. Fire crews are urged to exercise
extra caution when executing any prescribed burns, especially
where fuels, topography, and weather align.

